  "final_text": "I finished all four steps, and reading back the dashboard and the rule confirms the changes are in place.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' uses the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`, and its time field is `timestamp`. The fields used below are `Carrier` (string) and `Cancelled` (boolean).\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-2a50ef65 Flights' (id `s4-2a50ef65-flights-08220a05edaa`) with two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart counting flights by `timestamp`.\n   - A bar chart counting flights for the top 10 carriers.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-2a50ef65 Cancelled flights' (id `522e92f6-fcd0-44f0-ab8a-eef74872d848`) as an Elasticsearch query rule and turned it on when creating it. It runs every minute, matches flights where `Cancelled` is true over the last 5 minutes of `timestamp`, and fires when there are more than 10.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the carrier panel to \"Flights by carrier\", and the dashboard now shows that title. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
